<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christoph Froschauer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1490–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christoph Froschauer was a Bavarian-born printer who became the leading publisher of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in Zürich. In 1522 a meal of sausages in his house during Lent, defended by Zwingli, opened the break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His press printed the works of Zwingli and Bullinger and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zurich-bible">Zürich Bible</a>, completed in 1531, and made Zürich a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christoph-froschauer.jpg" alt="Christoph Froschauer" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Froschau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
